ruct\/keyed(by:resolvingConflictsWith:)","kind":"symbol","abstract":[],"type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/incidencematrixmodule\/orientedincidencematrix\/hyperedges-swift.struct\/keyed(by:resolvingconflictswith:)","title":"keyed(by:resolvingConflictsWith:)","role":"symbol"},"doc://IncidenceMatrixModule/documentation/IncidenceMatrixModule/OrientedIncidenceMatrix":{"title":"OrientedIncidenceMatrix","kind":"symbol","role":"symbol","abstract":[{"text":"The oriented incidence matrix of a directed hypergraph: entry (v, p) is −1 when ","type":"text"},{"code":"v","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" is a source","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"of column ","type":"text"},{"code":"p","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", +1 when it is a target, and 0 otherwise. Gallo, Longo, Pallottino and Nguyen","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"(1993) define a directed hypergraph’s matrix this way (aᵢⱼ = −1 for a tail node, 1 for a head","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"node); a directed graph’s edge is the column with one source and one target, the matrix of","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"spectral graph theory (B·Bᵀ = L, Godsil–Royle §8.3) and NetworkX’s","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"incidence_matrix(oriented=True)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":". It conforms to ","type":"text"},{"code":"DirectedHypergraph","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", so the ","type":"text"},{"code":"Hypergraphs","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"algorithms (B- and F-connection, components, the clique expansion) run on it unchanged; it is","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"not a ","type":"text"},{"code":"DirectedGraph","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":".","type":"text"}],"fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"OrientedIncidenceMatrix","kind":"identifier"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"OrientedIncidenceMatrix","kind":"identifier"}],"type":"topic","url":"\/docum